2026-10-25T03:20:00Z | manual | G1: supervisor decision recorded — 1.4 split into 1.4a COPIED (zero tolerance, unchanged) and 1.4b DERIVED-TRANSCENDENTAL (route predicate keyed to the documented `-exp(A_log)` route only). Implemented as a predicate, not a tolerance: no ulp knob, no epsilon constant; the bound is one `nextafter` hop from the value two independent correctly-rounded routes agree on, and a disagreement between those routes fails the case outright. Q5_K width fix authorised for `gguf_header_types.py` + `quant_scenarios.py`, with the instruction to derive widths from the pinned header rather than swap one typed constant for another.
2026-10-25T03:20:30Z | manual | G2: new tool `tools/ggml_block_geom.py` derives (elts/block, bytes/block, bpw) from `engines/llama.cpp/ggml/src/ggml-common.h` at run time — member-size sums including inline `union { struct {...} }` accessors, cross-checked against each struct's own `static_assert`. **It immediately refuted my own claim: Q5_K is 176 B/256 = 5.5 bpw, so the drift over 37 tensors is 74.00 MiB, not the ~232 MiB my previous summary asserted.** Widths for `gguf_reader.py`, `gguf_header_types.py`, `quant_scenarios.py` and the audit now come from here.
2026-10-25T03:22:00Z | manual | G3: `--selftest` gained witnesses for the new predicate, because the first version of `f32_nextafter` inverted its own bit-mapping and made "within one step" mean "within 3.8" — a bound that could not fail. The witness checks nextafter/ulp against hand-worked binary neighbours (2**-23 on [1,2), 2**-24 on [0.5,1), 2**-22 on [2,4)), checks a step measured by spacing equals a step measured in bits, and probes the predicate at 1 step (must stay clean), 2 steps, sign flip, permutation, both binade edges, and a reference disagreement (must fire). All OK; the real `blk.1.ssm_a` element is 1 step away under the corrected bound, same verdict, now for the right reason.
2026-10-25T03:24:00Z | manual | G4: `--selfcheck-widths` added and run — four negative controls, each must abort: a stale hand-typed row diffed against the header, a 2 B/block geometry error, the width-map tool facing a lying file size, and a policy row literal that is no derived width. 4/4 fired. `--emit` also gained a row-count witness, which caught two of its own bugs (a missing key and an off-by-two expectation) before it could write a short evidence file.
2026-10-25T03:27:00Z | manual | G5: full re-run in PLAN order from a fresh selftest PASS: selftest, 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.4 (1.4a 25 cases 0 outside; 1.4b 2 cases, 64 elements, 63 bit-exact + 1 within-route, 0 outside, 0 reference disagreements), floor probe, dequant, injections (150 band + 14 route cases, 0 wrong), shared, width controls, summary. 1.4 verdict is PASS-with-finding and the zero-tolerance FAIL on `blk.1.ssm_a` is retained beside it in `audit-exactness-2026-10-26.json`. No timing taken anywhere; CPU only.
